{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>My path</b> — He mentioned sitting and rising, and he mentioned walking, which is 'my path.' And behold, it will be fourth, like 'when I lie down,' for these four are mentioned in the Shema. And R. Moses the Kohen said: רבעי is Aramaic for 'my lying down,' and the meaning of זרית is from זר זהב (spreading gold), and it is from the heavy [piel] stem with a dagesh, and the sense is as though You spread gold [over] the place of my lying down and around all my paths. And the word הסכנת is a verb from the root הסכן, 'I am accustomed,' and the sense is like 'You have accustomed.' And the word 'my ways' — they are the ways of natural faculties: the eye for seeing, the ear for hearing, the palate for tasting, the tongue for speaking, the nose for smelling, and the entire body for touch; and likewise the function of every organ is a known thing, which no other organ performs. And those wise in natural science will understand this. And behold, this is like 'He who forms the eye — shall He not see?'"], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 139, :notes {0 "The verse lemma cited as ארחי ('my path') corresponds to Psalms 139:3; the comment also addresses זרית and סביבתי from the same verse. The referent of 'the four mentioned in the Shema' is the four body-postures in Deut. 6:7. R. Moses the Kohen is presumably Ibn Ezra's contemporary Moses ha-Kohen."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:42:08.215129479Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}